# Summary
Introduced DoS risk: batch delete bypasses existing object-key validation and processes unbounded key sizes/entries in one request, amplifying CPU/memory usage and blocking the server loop.
The new DeleteObjects handler parses XML keys into DeleteObjectEntry without applying the standard object-key validation (1–1024 bytes, no nulls). The coordinator then clones and processes these unvalidated keys for each deletion. Because request bodies can be large and the server handles requests serially, a single authenticated request containing extremely large keys or many entries can cause excessive hashing/DB work and large response construction, resulting in a denial of service.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Identify object-key validation rules and where they are enforced for URL-based operations (router.rs:59-114).
- [x] Verify DeleteObjects XML parser accepts <Key> contents without length/NUL validation (xml.rs:214-263).
- [x] Confirm DeleteObjects request path bypasses validator and feeds parsed keys directly to coordinator (http/mod.rs:145-148; coordinator.rs:604-633).
- [x] Check response generation embeds keys, allowing large outputs with many entries (xml.rs:275-304).
- [ ] Dynamic reproduction (build + run/ASan/valgrind/debugger) completed; blocked by missing crates.io dependencies and lack of valgrind/gdb.
## Report
Build-based validation was attempted but blocked by missing dependencies. `cargo build -p server --bin delete_poc` failed because crates.io access is blocked (tiny_http download 403), and `cargo build --offline` reported no matching package. Valgrind and gdb are not installed (`valgrind: command not found`, `gdb: command not found`), so crash/valgrind/debugger validation could not proceed.

Code review confirms the DoS risk: the object-key validator enforces 1–1024 bytes/no NULs in the router (crates/server/src/http/router.rs:59-72) and is only applied to URL keys (router.rs:111-114). The DeleteObjects handler bypasses this and directly parses body XML without validating key length or NULs (crates/server/src/http/xml.rs:214-263) and the HTTP dispatch path calls it directly (crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:145-148). The coordinator then iterates entries, clones each key into DeletedObject/DeleteError results (crates/server/src/coordinator.rs:604-633), and the response builder embeds the keys into XML (crates/server/src/http/xml.rs:275-304), so large keys/many entries inflate CPU/memory usage. The main server loop is explicitly serial (crates/server/src/main.rs:97-99), which increases the availability impact of a single heavy request. These code paths support the finding that unbounded DeleteObjects requests can cause a DoS via oversized or numerous keys.

