# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
The issue is a real, reachable DoS in the main HTTP service: DeleteObjects XML keys bypass validation and are processed in a serial request loop, enabling an authenticated client to consume CPU/memory. The body size cap (~256MB) and requirement for valid SigV4 credentials reduce blast radius and ease, so impact is availability-only and bounded to a single instance. This aligns with medium severity rather than high/critical.
## Likelihood
high - Requires valid credentials and a large request body; achievable by any authenticated client if the service is network-reachable and no stricter proxy limits exist. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Availability impact: large authenticated DeleteObjects requests can monopolize CPU/memory and block the single-threaded server loop, degrading service for all clients on that instance.
## Assumptions
- The argmin-s3 service is deployed with ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR bound to a network-reachable address (not only 127.0.0.1).
- Attacker can obtain valid SigV4 credentials (access key/secret) for the service.
- No external reverse proxy enforces stricter request body or per-request object limits than the server itself.
- Valid SigV4 credentials (access key/secret)
- Network reachability to the HTTP listener
- Ability to submit a large POST /<bucket>?delete request body (<=256MB)
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4 -> n5 (DoS)
## Path evidence
- `crates/server/src/http/router.rs:59-114` - Object-key validation exists (1–1024 bytes, no NULs) and is applied to URL path keys only.
- `crates/server/src/http/xml.rs:214-258` - DeleteObjects XML parser extracts <Key> values without any length or NUL validation.
- `crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:46-148` - All requests are authenticated, then DeleteObjects dispatch directly calls parse_delete_objects_xml and coordinator.delete_objects.
- `crates/server/src/coordinator.rs:604-629` - delete_objects iterates entries and clones each key into DeletedObject/DeleteError results.
- `crates/server/src/http/request.rs:6-88` - Request body size is capped at ~256MB, limiting total XML size but not per-key or per-entry counts.
- `crates/server/src/main.rs:97-99` - Server handles requests serially, so a heavy DeleteObjects request blocks other clients.
- `crates/server/src/config.rs:19-41` - Listen address and credentials are configured via environment; default bind is 127.0.0.1:9000 and requires access key/secret.
## Narrative
The DeleteObjects handler parses XML <Key> values without enforcing the existing 1–1024 byte/no-NUL object-key validation used for URL keys, then passes these entries directly to coordinator.delete_objects, which iterates and clones each key for deletion and response construction. With a single-threaded request loop, an authenticated client can submit a large DeleteObjects body (up to the 256MB body limit) containing oversized or many keys, causing excessive CPU/memory work and blocking other requests, resulting in a service-level DoS. Authentication is required, and a body size cap exists, but there is no per-entry limit or key-length validation in this path.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication required for all requests
- Request body size cap (~256MB)
- URL object-key validation (not applied to DeleteObjects body)
## Blindspots
- No dynamic testing of performance impact; actual CPU/memory saturation depends on runtime environment and request sizes.
- Deployment exposure is configuration-dependent; if bound to localhost or protected by a proxy with strict limits, exploitability is reduced.
- Unknown whether operational rate limiting or WAF rules are applied in real deployments.
